  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nThe cold front is currently draped across southeast Ohio and is\npushing further into our area moving slowly southward through \nthis afternoon and evening. As of now, light shower activity is\nprevalent across much of the northwest area in our CWA. No \nthunderstorms have been observed at the moment, however we \ncannot rule out some isolated instances of a thunderstorm which\nmay lead to stronger winds gusts than what is out there \nalready. SPS worthy winds gusts will likely not be the case and\nthe severe threat is nil due to low instability and the lack of \nstrong wind shear. \n\nWPC has us under a marginal threat for excessive rainfall which\ncould promote very isolated and localized instances of flash \nflooding really only across flood or nuisance prone areas under\npossible heavier downpours. The area has been dry and flash \nflood guidance is on the high side elsewhere so the threat is\nconfined to those aforementioned areas.\n\nThis shower and possible storm activity will go into the \nevening as the front finally sags to our south for a clean \nfrontal passage, however the front will meander about to our \nsouth providing additional chances for shower or storm activity\nfor tonight and for Saturday. This potential activity will be \nmainly confined to the southernmost portions of West Virginia \nand southwest Virginia and will be sub-severe in nature. WPC \nalso has us under a marginal threat for excessive rainfall so \nagain, some isolated instances of flash flooding are possible \nunder flood prone or nuisance areas. \n\nA wave of low pressure is then forecast to ride along the \nboundary to our south and bring in more chances for shower \nand/or thunderstorms for Sunday morning albeit sub-severe in \nnature once again. WPC again has us under a marginal however to\na slight threat for the extreme southern portions of our CWA \nfor Sunday, although any activity should be exiting by early \nSunday evening.\n\nThe aforementioned system moves away from the area as another \nsystem, a broad clipper type system, brings a reinforcing cold \nfrontal boundary through the area the rest of Sunday. This new \nfeature will push the meandering wave out of the area and \nprovide stronger high pressure building in from the north. This \ncold frontal boundary will be moisture starved and provide \ndrier and cooler weather to start the new work week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nWith the main weather maker exiting toward the south by Monday\nmorning, strong high pressure builds in and advects in cold air\nwhich will maintain temperatures down to below seasonable for \nthe new work week. This high pressure system will provide a \nbreak from unsettled weather for the rest of the work week and \npossibly beyond. \n\nAs the cooler and quieter weather sets in for the new work \nweek the potential for frost across the northeast mountains \ncould occur on early Tuesday and Wednesday mornings. \nTemperatures should then moderate some for the second half of \nthe work week to where the threat for frost will diminish.",
